{question}

{choices}

Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: "Company Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, occurrence, or development that, individually or in combination with other effects, has a material adverse impact on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, or operations of the Company. Notwithstanding any adverse developments, the following shall not be deemed to constitute, or be taken into account in determining whether a Company Material Adverse Effect has occurred: (i) general changes in economic conditions, fluctuations in financial and credit markets, or adjustments in governmental regulations that affect the industry at large; (ii) any adverse effects solely attributable to market volatility or routine operational adjustments; provided, however, that such exclusions do not extend to delays or impairments that have a direct and material impact on the Company’s ability to consummate the Transaction unless those delays have been explicitly approved in writing by Parent; (iii) any judicial or administrative orders not arising from the Company’s negligence; and (iv) any events described in (i) or (ii) which are de minimis in nature. Further, the language herein makes clear that while many adverse conditions are excluded from being considered as material adverse effects, there is no express carveout for issues that directly affect the ability to consummate the Transaction, and any material delay or impairment in consummation remains fully within the ambit of a Company Material Adverse Effect.\n
Choices:
